ABSTRACT_EN = [
'Sensor-fault handling in automated driving stacks largely relies on a single strategy — an immediate in-lane stop — which leaves the vehicle obstructing traffic, while undetected faults let the vehicle keep driving with degraded perception. This thesis proposes a graded fault-response framework consisting of (1) a behavior-tree-based monitor (cdr_monitor) that estimates per-sensor health as continuous scores from freshness, quality, and physics channels, and (2) a supervisor (mrm_supervisor) that selects between shoulder/parking-lot evacuation (MRM_MOVE) and deferral to the stock stop (MRM_DEFER) according to fault type, severity, and remaining capability, and enforces a conservative fixed speed cap (3.0 m/s, below the analytically recoverable speed of about 3.89 m/s derived from a stopping-distance model d = vτ + v²/2a) during evacuation; a health-proportional cap is implemented but was not used in the reported experiments.',
'The framework was evaluated in a closed loop of Autoware 0.39.0 and CARLA 0.9.16 (Town07, 15 km/h speed limit). A matrix of 1,131 valid cases across fault types, severities, traffic densities, and methods verified the monitoring and evacuation behavior: the detector achieved 96-100% detection rates with 2.1-5.0 s median detection latency in 11 of 12 fault conditions (the exception being a by-design tolerated mild lidar sparsification) with zero false activations in 45 fault-free cases, and supervision reduced degraded-state driving exposure from 238-275 m (unsupervised stock) to 32-58 m (detect + evacuate, camera and localization faults). In the ablation sample, the in-lane stop kept a median post-encounter moving exposure of 0.0 m while occupying the driving lane beyond the observation horizon, whereas evacuation spent a median of 37.2 m of controlled low-speed motion. Among the 20 evacuation-arm cases (seeds S02-S11, arms A3/A4; non-ablation cases — development seeds and ladder runs — and post-collision departures were excluded by the inclusion criteria defined in the analysis rules of Chapter 4), 7 collided and are reported separately as failed lane clearance, 6 cleared the driving lane within the observation horizon (observed median 11.0 s), and 7 did not clear within the horizon (right-censored); with clearance observed in 6 of 13 collision-free cases, the Kaplan-Meier survival curve does not fall below 0.5 (terminal survival 0.538), so the median clearance time is not reached within the horizon. Safety was quantified at the encounter level with a triple collision-verdict system — a real-time ego collision-sensor ledger (RAW-HIT), a walker-physics oracle, and a human visual cross-check protocol — introduced after empirically discovering that a walker-attached collision sensor never fired; the pitfalls of runner exit labels and official infraction records are documented as a methodological contribution.',
'Distance-ladder experiments located the avoid/collide boundary at 8-11 m for 4.2 m/s driving (consistent with the stopping-distance model prediction of 8.8 m) and at 7-8 m for 3.0 m/s. In a paired same-seed four-arm ablation (20 seeds over two identically-protocoled rounds, pedestrian dart-out at 8 m launched at detection+3 s, i.e., during the deceleration transient), native continuation collided in 14/20 seeds, detection with in-lane stop in 0/20, uncapped evacuation in 10/20, and capped (3.0 m/s) evacuation in 11/20 (exact McNemar, A1 vs A4: p=0.375; no additional collisions were prevented by the explicit cap in the tested sample — not a claim of equivalence). Crucially, the same capped-evacuation policy in settled cruise after deceleration avoided all encounters (0/8). The MRM choice is thus a temporal trade-off between immediate collision risk (favoring the stop) and the possibility, when evacuation succeeds, of clearing the lane to a safer final position, and the residual risk of evacuation concentrates in the detection-to-deceleration transient (11/20 transient vs 0/8 settled) — an observational two-regime comparison, not a causal manipulation of detection timing, that nevertheless provides quantitative observational evidence supporting the need for earlier detection and faster deceleration in shortening the fault-tolerant time interval (FTTI). A latency audit further showed that most of the reported hard-failure detection delay was decision inertia and measurement distortion rather than sensing: an evidence-aware fast path (skipping noise-oriented debounce and futile recovery holds for confirmed sensor death, with precomputed candidate routes) brought detection-to-decision within a 1.5-s deadline across a full re-collection, measured in simulation time to exclude actor-destruction stalls. A supplementary campaign extended the fault model to correlated GNSS bias, GNSS outage, and IMU bias/drift faults (611 valid cases, 100% detection in all conditions). Limitations include the single map, the single 15 km/h speed limit, two launch corridors, and the version-specific scope of all Autoware observations.',
]
ABSTRACT_KEYWORDS = 'Keywords: autonomous driving, functional safety, minimal risk maneuver, FTTI, sensor fault detection, behavior tree'
